  "query_preview": "..Dry and seasonably warm weather continues today. The \nridge that was over the Four Corners starts to recenter, in response \nto a very weak trough approaching the west coast. Boundary layer high \npressure to our east will maintain easterly winds, but the loss of \nupper level support means speeds will start to decrease.\n\nBy Tuesday, the trough moves through northern Arizona and then \nmeanders through eastern Arizona on Wednesday and Thursday. This \nwill allow the atmosphere to destabilize just enough to introduce a \nhigh-based convective threat Tuesday, Wednesday and maybe Thursday.  \n\nThe ridge then briefly rebuilds later in the week, before possibly \nbreaking down again next weekend, as a much more significant trough \nforms over the interior west.\n\nIt is next weekend or early the following week that may bring more \nsignificant weather to our area. This pattern bears watching, as \nsome models entrain tropical moisture and perhaps even a tropical \ncirculation. As a result, cooler temperatures and potentially a \nreturn of unsettled conditions will be possible near the end of \nthe forecast period.\n\nUncertainty remains high with this, thus confidence in any exact \ndetails remains low.",
